WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.988
Monitoring and threat hunting are related but distinct security functions.

00:00:03.988 --> 00:00:07.877
Monitoring continuously reviews events and alerts generated by security systems.

00:00:07.877 --> 00:00:12.543
It helps teams recognize known conditions and respond consistently at operational scale.

00:00:12.543 --> 00:00:16.820
Monitoring commonly relies on defined rules, analytics, thresholds, and correlation logic.

00:00:16.820 --> 00:00:21.486
When those conditions are met, an alert enters a queue for triage.

00:00:21.486 --> 00:00:26.152
This workflow is reactive to a signal produced by the monitoring system.

00:00:26.152 --> 00:00:29.262
Threat hunting is proactive and usually hypothesis driven.

00:00:29.262 --> 00:00:34.706
A hunter begins with a defensible question, identifies the data needed to explore it,

00:00:34.706 --> 00:00:39.761
examines evidence, and records whether the observations support or challenge the original idea.

00:00:39.761 --> 00:00:43.260
A hunt is not an unstructured search through logs.

00:00:43.260 --> 00:00:46.371
Scope, assumptions, data sources, time boundaries, analytical steps,

00:00:46.371 --> 00:00:51.814
and expected outcomes should be documented so the work can be reviewed and repeated.

00:00:51.814 --> 00:00:54.147
The two functions reinforce one another.

00:00:54.147 --> 00:00:58.036
Monitoring data gives hunters a starting point and historical context.

00:00:58.036 --> 00:01:00.758
Hunts can identify new patterns, data requirements,

00:01:00.758 --> 00:01:05.035
or analytical opportunities that detection engineers turn into repeatable monitoring capabilities.

00:01:05.035 --> 00:01:08.534
When a hunt identifies credible evidence of harmful activity,

00:01:08.534 --> 00:01:12.034
the work may transition into incident triage or response.

00:01:12.034 --> 00:01:15.144
That handoff should follow defined procedures, preserve context,

00:01:15.144 --> 00:01:18.644
and involve the teams responsible for managing the event.

00:01:18.644 --> 00:01:20.977
Monitoring is designed for persistent coverage,

00:01:20.977 --> 00:01:24.087
while hunting uses analyst attention to explore uncertainty.

00:01:24.087 --> 00:01:27.976
Neither should be treated as a replacement for the other,

00:01:27.976 --> 00:01:32.642
and neither eliminates the need for incident response or other security controls.

00:01:32.642 --> 00:01:37.308
A mature security operation creates a feedback loop: monitoring reveals operational patterns,

00:01:37.308 --> 00:01:39.641
hunting tests difficult questions, detections improve,

00:01:39.641 --> 00:01:43.140
and incident lessons generate new hypotheses for future hunts.
